11:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L20",null,{"guide":{"date":"July 31–August 25, 1862","place":"Chattanooga and Knoxville in Tennessee; Cumberland Gap, Rogers Gap, Barbourville, London, Big Hill, Richmond, Lexington, and the Kentucky River line in Kentucky","result":"Edmund Kirby Smith abandoned the preliminary reduction of Cumberland Gap and led the newly styled Confederate Army of Kentucky north from Knoxville. By bypassing the gap, detaching Carter Stevenson to contain George W. Morgan, and advancing through Barbourville and London, Smith cut the United States garrison’s communications and opened the Old State Road toward Richmond and Lexington. The Confederate movement forced a hurried United States reorganization in Kentucky, but John Hunt Morgan’s promise of mass recruiting proved false in the mountain counties. At the episode’s end Smith had resumed his advance with Patrick Cleburne and Thomas Churchill while Henry Heth remained near Barbourville and Stevenson watched Cumberland Gap","facts":[{"label":"Episode scope","value":"Kirby Smith turns the Kentucky plan into a separate field campaign","note":"The episode begins with the July 31 Chattanooga conference and follows Smith’s advance through August 25, immediately before the Battle of Richmond."},{"label":"Original Confederate sequence","value":"Reduce Cumberland Gap, join Bragg, then move against Nashville","note":"Smith initially agreed to attack George W. Morgan’s garrison with about 20,000 men, including formations borrowed from Bragg, before combining for an operation in Middle Tennessee."},{"label":"Command relationship","value":"Cooperation between separate departments","note":"Braxton Bragg outranked Smith, but Smith commanded the independent Department of East Tennessee. Bragg could not exercise ordinary continuous command over Smith while their forces operated apart."},{"label":"Kirby Smith in 1862","value":"Age 38; West Point class of 1845","note":"The Florida-born officer had served in Mexico, taught mathematics at West Point, and joined the 2nd U.S. Cavalry before resigning when Florida seceded."},{"label":"Correct term: brevetted","value":"Smith received brevet promotions for gallantry in Mexico","note":"The automated transcript says he was “brevity.” He was brevetted for Cerro Gordo and for Contreras and Churubusco."},{"label":"First Manassas","value":"Wounded while leading a late Confederate arrival","note":"Smith took command of his brigade after arriving from the Shenandoah Valley and was wounded during the Confederate counterattack. His performance strengthened his public reputation."},{"label":"East Tennessee assignment","value":"Military defense and internal control","note":"Smith assumed command in East Tennessee in early 1862. He guarded a strategically important railroad region with a large pro-Union population."},{"label":"John Hunt Morgan’s July raid","value":"About 900 cavalrymen and an exaggerated recruiting forecast","note":"Morgan reported that 25,000 or 30,000 Kentuckians would join a Confederate advance. Smith’s experience in the mountain counties quickly contradicted that claim."},{"label":"Cumberland Gap garrison","value":"About 9,000 United States troops under George W. Morgan","note":"The position was supplied well enough to resist a quick reduction. A prolonged siege would have destroyed the speed on which the proposed Nashville operation depended."},{"label":"Smith’s August 9 proposal","value":"Bypass the gap and move directly toward Lexington","note":"Smith argued that a northward advance would isolate Morgan while producing more useful strategic and political results than a slow investment of the pass."},{"label":"Change of objective","value":"Kentucky and Lexington replace Middle Tennessee and Nashville","note":"Bragg accepted Smith’s proposal rather than enforcing the original sequence. The decision left the western offensive divided between commanders and routes."},{"label":"Lexington’s appeal","value":"Political symbol, supply center, and road hub","note":"The city was associated with Henry Clay, John C. Breckinridge, and Mary Todd Lincoln. Confederate planners also expected supplies and recruits in the Bluegrass region."},{"label":"Army name","value":"Confederate Army of Kentucky","note":"Smith adopted the name before the advance. Later in the episode, the emergency United States force forming around Lexington received the same name."},{"label":"Departure from Knoxville","value":"August 14, 1862","note":"Smith led the main force north into the Cumberland Mountains while Bragg continued assembling the Army of Mississippi at Chattanooga."},{"label":"Entry into Kentucky","value":"Rogers Gap, August 16","note":"The episode’s route is Rogers Gap, not Cumberland Gap. Smith crossed west of Morgan’s fortified position and moved toward Barbourville."},{"label":"First Division","value":"Brig. Gen. Carter L. Stevenson; roughly 9,000 men","note":"The largest division was detached to approach and contain the United States garrison at Cumberland Gap."},{"label":"Second Division","value":"Brig. Gen. Henry Heth; roughly 6,000 men","note":"The automated transcript renders Heth as “Heath.” His division remained near Barbourville when Smith resumed the advance on August 25."},{"label":"Third Division","value":"Brig. Gen. Thomas J. Churchill; roughly 3,000 men","note":"Churchill temporarily commanded in place of Maj. Gen. John P. McCown, who stayed in Knoxville with responsibility for East Tennessee during Smith’s absence."},{"label":"Fourth Division","value":"Brig. Gen. Patrick R. Cleburne; roughly 3,000 men","note":"The command consisted of two brigades lent by Bragg. The automated transcript renders Cleburne as “Claiborne.”"},{"label":"Cavalry","value":"Col. John S. Scott; roughly 1,000 men","note":"Scott screened the advance, ranged north along the Old State Road, and reported that the road toward Lexington appeared open."},{"label":"Strength figures","value":"Approximate and internally inconsistent","note":"The episode calls Smith’s army about 20,000, while its rounded divisional and cavalry estimates add to about 22,000. The figures are best read as broad estimates, not a precise return."},{"label":"Immediate operational aim","value":"Cut Morgan’s supply line to Lexington","note":"Stevenson fixed the gap garrison while the main body moved northwest through Barbourville and London, placing itself between Morgan and central Kentucky."},{"label":"Campaign weather","value":"Extreme heat and a prolonged drought","note":"Dust, scarce water, and difficult mountain roads burdened the march and limited the supplies the Confederates expected to gather locally."},{"label":"Local response","value":"Hostile in the mountain counties","note":"Smith wrote that the people were bitterly opposed to his army. The early march supplied no evidence for Morgan’s prediction of tens of thousands of recruits."},{"label":"Barbourville and London","value":"Small United States detachments driven off","note":"The Confederates used superior numbers to clear the route. These actions were not major battles, but they helped isolate Cumberland Gap."},{"label":"Cumberland Gap isolated","value":"By August 20","note":"Stevenson contained Morgan while Smith’s other divisions occupied the roads to the garrison’s rear. The United States force remained in the gap during the episode."},{"label":"Big Hill","value":"Scott’s cavalry routed a raw United States force on August 23","note":"The action opened the Old State Road north toward Richmond and encouraged Smith to continue the advance."},{"label":"United States vulnerability","value":"Few trained troops between Smith and Lexington","note":"The 18th Kentucky Infantry guarded the railroad corridor, while new regiments from Ohio and Indiana arrived with little training."},{"label":"Department of the Ohio reorganized","value":"Maj. Gen. Horatio G. Wright placed in command","note":"General in Chief Henry W. Halleck responded to Smith’s advance by expanding Wright’s authority as reinforcements moved into Kentucky."},{"label":"Lew Wallace’s defensive plan","value":"Hold the Kentucky River crossings south of Lexington","note":"Wallace and Cassius M. Clay considered the high river bluffs and limited crossings a strong defensive line."},{"label":"Correct name: Cassius Marcellus Clay","value":"Kentucky abolitionist and United States major general","note":"The episode calls him “Marcellus Clay.” Cassius M. Clay was in Kentucky on a mission for Lincoln and advised Wallace using his knowledge of the region."},{"label":"Correct nickname: Bull Nelson","value":"Maj. Gen. William “Bull” Nelson","note":"The automated transcript twice renders the nickname as “Buell.” Nelson was a former U.S. naval officer and Army of the Ohio division commander."},{"label":"Correct names: Cruft and Mahlon Manson","value":"Brig. Gens. Charles Cruft and Mahlon D. Manson","note":"The automated transcript gives “Croft” and “Malen.” Nelson assigned each an infantry brigade in the improvised United States Army of Kentucky."},{"label":"Nelson takes control","value":"Replaced Wallace at Lexington and pushed infantry south","note":"Nelson sent Manson toward Richmond, assigned the cavalry to James S. Jackson, and rejected the more cautious Kentucky River defense."},{"label":"Smith’s August 25 movement","value":"Cleburne, Churchill, and Scott advance toward Richmond","note":"Stevenson remained at Cumberland Gap and Heth near Barbourville. Smith therefore approached the next battle with only part of his total army immediately available."},{"label":"Recommended book","value":"“General Edmund Kirby Smith, C.S.A.” by Joseph H. Parks","note":"The episode recommends Parks’s biography while noting that it is one of relatively few book-length lives of Smith."}],"maps":[{"title":"Kirby Smith’s road into Kentucky","caption":"This schematic follows Smith from Knoxville through Rogers Gap, Barbourville, London, Big Hill, and toward Richmond and Lexington. It also shows the operational split: Stevenson contained Cumberland Gap, Heth stayed near Barbourville, and Cleburne, Churchill, and Scott continued north.","source":"Custom diagram based on the U.S. Army staff ride handbook, National Park Service, Kentucky Historical Society, and episode transcript; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep207-kirby-smith-kentucky-route.svg","alt":"Schematic map of Edmund Kirby Smith’s August 1862 advance from Knoxville through Rogers Gap and Barbourville toward Richmond and Lexington, with Confederate detachments at Cumberland Gap and Barbourville."},{"title":"United States operations at Cumberland Gap","caption":"Historical map prepared by the U.S. Army Corps of Engineers and Edward Ruger. It shows roads, relief, fortifications, and operations associated with George W. Morgan’s division during 1862. It is a detailed local map, not a map of Smith’s full route to Lexington.","source":"Library of Congress Geography and Map Division and Library of Virginia; free to use and reuse; see source record below.","image":"/images/ep207-cumberland-gap-operations-map.jpg","alt":"Historical military map of Cumberland Gap showing roads, ridges, fortifications, and troop movements during George W. Morgan’s 1862 operations."},{"title":"Edmund Kirby Smith","caption":"Wartime glass-negative portrait of the independent East Tennessee commander whose decision to bypass Cumberland Gap set the campaign’s eastern column in motion.","source":"Civil War Glass Negatives and Related Prints, Library of Congress; no known restrictions on publication; see source record below.","image":"/images/ep207-edmund-kirby-smith.jpg","alt":"Wartime photographic portrait of Confederate Major General Edmund Kirby Smith in uniform."},{"title":"Patrick R. Cleburne","caption":"Historical engraved portrait of Cleburne, commander of the borrowed 4th Division and one of the two division leaders who continued toward Richmond with Smith on August 25.","source":"Civil War Photograph Collection, Library of Congress; no known restrictions on publication; see source record below.","image":"/images/ep207-patrick-cleburne.jpg","alt":"Engraved head-and-shoulders portrait of Confederate Brigadier General Patrick R. Cleburne facing left."}],"moments":[{"time":"1:16","audioTime":76.5,"title":"Buell’s advance remains tied to the railroad","detail":"Don Carlos Buell repairs and guards the Memphis and Charleston Railroad while Confederate cavalry threatens the long supply connection back to Louisville."},{"time":"1:44","audioTime":104.38,"title":"Bragg shifts the main Confederate army east","detail":"The successful long-distance movement from northern Mississippi places roughly 30,000 Confederates at Chattanooga before Buell can reach the city."},{"time":"2:18","audioTime":138.26,"title":"Bragg and Smith meet at Chattanooga","detail":"The two major generals discuss cooperation between their separate commands on July 31."},{"time":"3:23","audioTime":202.52,"title":"Smith presses for a preliminary attack on Cumberland Gap","detail":"He argues that George W. Morgan must be removed before he can join Bragg for an advance against Nashville."},{"time":"3:55","audioTime":234.56,"title":"Smith reconsiders the agreement","detail":"After returning to Knoxville, he begins moving away from the plan to reduce the gap before joining Bragg."},{"time":"4:18","audioTime":257.52,"title":"The episode introduces Edmund Kirby Smith","detail":"Smith is 38, Florida-born, and a graduate of the United States Military Academy’s class of 1845."},{"time":"5:05","audioTime":305.18,"title":"Smith resigns after Florida secedes","detail":"Although personally opposed to secession, he leaves the United States Army and accepts a Confederate commission."},{"time":"5:33","audioTime":333.4,"title":"First Manassas establishes Smith’s reputation","detail":"His late-arriving brigade joins the Confederate counterattack; Smith is wounded but emerges with increased fame."},{"time":"6:08","audioTime":367.74,"title":"East Tennessee offers responsibility but less glory","detail":"Smith must defend the region and control a substantial pro-Union population from his Knoxville headquarters."},{"time":"6:31","audioTime":391.18,"title":"Kentucky becomes the tempting objective","detail":"Political appeals and the prospect of independent success pull Smith away from the Nashville plan."},{"time":"7:01","audioTime":421.02,"title":"John Hunt Morgan predicts mass recruitment","detail":"After his July raid, Morgan reports that 25,000 or 30,000 Kentuckians will join a Confederate invasion."},{"time":"7:29","audioTime":449.34,"title":"Cumberland Gap cannot be reduced quickly","detail":"George W. Morgan’s roughly 9,000-man garrison is too strong and well supplied for the short operation Bragg’s timetable requires."},{"time":"8:11","audioTime":490.78,"title":"Lexington offers political and material rewards","detail":"The Bluegrass city promises supplies, symbolic value, and—in Confederate estimates—new recruits."},{"time":"8:26","audioTime":506.5,"title":"Smith proposes the direct move on August 9","detail":"He tells Bragg that an advance toward Lexington can isolate Morgan without spending weeks against Cumberland Gap."},{"time":"9:14","audioTime":553.98,"title":"Bragg accepts Kentucky as the objective","detail":"His reply permits Smith’s independent northern axis and shifts the campaign away from the earlier Nashville concentration."},{"time":"9:41","audioTime":581.18,"title":"Smith renames his command","detail":"The Army of East Tennessee becomes the Confederate Army of Kentucky as preparations for the invasion conclude."},{"time":"9:48","audioTime":587.94,"title":"The army leaves Knoxville","detail":"Smith leads the column north on August 14, beginning the Kentucky campaign."},{"time":"11:09","audioTime":669.32,"title":"The Confederates enter Kentucky through Rogers Gap","detail":"On August 16 Smith bypasses the fortified route through Cumberland Gap while Bragg remains at Chattanooga."},{"time":"11:23","audioTime":682.96,"title":"Four unequal divisions make up the field army","detail":"Stevenson commands the largest formation, followed by Heth, Churchill, and Cleburne, with Scott’s cavalry screening the movement."},{"time":"11:56","audioTime":715.96,"title":"Cleburne commands Bragg’s borrowed brigades","detail":"The Irish-born Arkansas officer leads the makeshift 4th Division of about 3,000 men."},{"time":"12:23","audioTime":742.76,"title":"The movement targets Morgan’s supply line","detail":"Stevenson approaches Cumberland Gap while the other divisions move toward Barbourville and London."},{"time":"12:41","audioTime":761.18,"title":"Drought shapes the march","detail":"The army enters a region suffering from months of dry weather, scarce water, oppressive heat, and dusty roads."},{"time":"13:13","audioTime":792.92,"title":"Smith reports local hostility","detail":"His letter from the mountain region contradicts the expectation that Kentuckians will immediately rally to the Confederacy."},{"time":"13:29","audioTime":808.56,"title":"Cumberland Gap is isolated","detail":"By August 20 Stevenson has Morgan pinned while Smith’s main body controls the roads toward central Kentucky."},{"time":"13:46","audioTime":825.96,"title":"Scott wins the skirmish at Big Hill","detail":"His cavalry drives away inexperienced United States horsemen on August 23 and scouts the road toward Richmond."},{"time":"14:11","audioTime":850.97,"title":"Smith’s advance shocks the United States command","detail":"The main force east of the Tennessee River is trapped at the gap, leaving few organized defenders between the Confederates and Lexington."},{"time":"14:59","audioTime":899.45,"title":"Halleck reorganizes the Department of the Ohio","detail":"Maj. Gen. Horatio G. Wright receives the expanded command as new Ohio and Indiana regiments move south."},{"time":"15:29","audioTime":929.39,"title":"A second Army of Kentucky forms at Lexington","detail":"The improvised United States force shares the name of Smith’s Confederate army and initially comes under Lew Wallace."},{"time":"15:51","audioTime":950.57,"title":"Wallace selects the Kentucky River line","detail":"He plans to defend the limited crossings and high bluffs south of Lexington with advice from Cassius M. Clay."},{"time":"16:34","audioTime":993.97,"title":"Buell sends commanders north","detail":"William Nelson, Charles Cruft, and Mahlon D. Manson leave the Army of the Ohio to help organize Kentucky’s defense."},{"time":"16:50","audioTime":1009.81,"title":"William “Bull” Nelson enters the crisis","detail":"The Kentucky-born former naval officer brings senior rank, local connections, and an aggressive temperament."},{"time":"17:54","audioTime":1074.05,"title":"Nelson replaces Wallace at Lexington","detail":"After quarreling with Wright in Louisville, Nelson assumes control and abandons Wallace’s command arrangement."},{"time":"18:14","audioTime":1094.33,"title":"Nelson reorganizes the field force","detail":"Cruft and Manson receive infantry brigades, while Brig. Gen. James S. Jackson takes the cavalry."},{"time":"18:28","audioTime":1108.13,"title":"The United States infantry moves south","detail":"Nelson orders Manson across the Kentucky River toward Richmond and sends the cavalry toward the Camp Dick Robinson supply area."},{"time":"18:44","audioTime":1123.65,"title":"Smith decides to press forward","detail":"Scott’s report that the way toward Lexington appears open confirms Smith’s intention to continue."},{"time":"19:06","audioTime":1145.81,"title":"Only part of Smith’s army moves on Richmond","detail":"Stevenson stays at Cumberland Gap and Heth at Barbourville while Cleburne, Churchill, and Scott advance."},{"time":"19:29","audioTime":1168.69,"title":"Smith compares the expedition to Cortés","detail":"Writing to his wife, he presents the deep independent advance as a gamble that could establish his military genius."},{"time":"19:49","audioTime":1189.49,"title":"The main column resumes the march","detail":"On August 25, in heat and drought, Smith sets out for Richmond and Lexington."},{"time":"20:10","audioTime":1209.98,"title":"The episode recommends Joseph H. Parks’s biography","detail":"“General Edmund Kirby Smith, C.S.A.” is presented as a useful, if imperfect, book-length study of the commander."}],"command":[{"side":"Confederate western coordination","color":"#963b31","branches":[{"commander":"Gen. Braxton Bragg","command":"Army of Mississippi and Department No. 2","subordinates":"Concentrated at Chattanooga, lent two brigades to Smith, and accepted the change from a Nashville concentration to separate advances into Kentucky"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Edmund Kirby Smith","command":"Department of East Tennessee and Army of Kentucky","subordinates":"Bypassed Cumberland Gap, divided his army to contain Morgan, and directed the main advance toward Richmond and Lexington"}]},{"side":"Confederate Army of Kentucky","color":"#a45a36","branches":[{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Carter L. Stevenson","command":"1st Division","subordinates":"Roughly 9,000 men; detached to approach and contain the United States garrison at Cumberland Gap"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Henry Heth","command":"2nd Division","subordinates":"Roughly 6,000 men; moved into Kentucky and remained near Barbourville when Smith advanced on August 25"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Thomas J. Churchill","command":"3rd Division","subordinates":"Roughly 3,000 men; temporarily replaced John P. McCown and continued toward Richmond with Smith"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Patrick R. Cleburne","command":"4th Division","subordinates":"Roughly 3,000 men in two brigades borrowed from Bragg; continued toward Richmond with Smith"},{"commander":"Col. John S. Scott","command":"Cavalry","subordinates":"Roughly 1,000 cavalrymen; screened the advance, fought at Big Hill, and scouted the Old State Road toward Richmond"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. John P. McCown","command":"East Tennessee in Smith’s absence","subordinates":"Remained at Knoxville rather than accompanying the 3rd Division that Churchill temporarily commanded"}]},{"side":"United States command in Kentucky","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Henry W. Halleck","command":"General in chief","subordinates":"Responded to the invasion by reorganizing authority in the Department of the Ohio under Horatio Wright"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Horatio G. Wright","command":"Department of the Ohio","subordinates":"Supervised the emergency reinforcement of Kentucky while disputing strategy and authority with William Nelson"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Jeremiah T. Boyle","command":"Military command in Kentucky","subordinates":"Warned higher authorities and requested reinforcements as Smith’s purpose became clear"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. George W. Morgan","command":"7th Division, Army of the Ohio","subordinates":"Held Cumberland Gap with roughly 9,000 troops but lost his route to Lexington when Smith moved around him"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Lew Wallace","command":"First commander, United States Army of Kentucky","subordinates":"Organized a defense along the Kentucky River before Nelson replaced him at Lexington"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. William “Bull” Nelson","command":"United States Army of Kentucky","subordinates":"Replaced Wallace; assigned infantry brigades to Charles Cruft and Mahlon Manson and cavalry to James S. Jackson; ordered the infantry toward Richmond"}]}],"people":[{"name":"Don Carlos Buell","role":"Army of the Ohio commander whose slow advance toward Chattanooga forms the strategic background to Smith’s opportunity","side":"United States","source":"https://history.army.mil/portals/143/Images/Publications/catalog/75-7.pdf"},{"name":"Henry W. Halleck","role":"United States general in chief who reorganized the Department of the Ohio in response to the Kentucky invasion","side":"United States","source":"https://www.armyupress.army.mil/Portals/7/educational-services/staff-rides/StaffRideHB_Perryville.pdf"},{"name":"Horatio G. Wright","role":"New Department of the Ohio commander responsible for the emergency defense of Kentucky","side":"United States","source":"https://www.armyupress.army.mil/Portals/7/educational-services/staff-rides/StaffRideHB_Perryville.pdf"},{"name":"Jeremiah T. Boyle","role":"United States military commander in Kentucky who appealed for troops as Smith advanced","side":"United States","source":"https://www.armyupress.army.mil/Portals/7/educational-services/staff-rides/StaffRideHB_Perryville.pdf"},{"name":"George W. Morgan","role":"7th Division commander isolated with roughly 9,000 troops at Cumberland Gap","side":"United States","source":"https://www.nps.gov/cuga/civil-war-at-cumberland-gap.htm"},{"name":"Lew Wallace","role":"First commander of the emergency United States Army of Kentucky and advocate of a Kentucky River defense","side":"United States","source":"https://www.armyupress.army.mil/Portals/7/educational-services/staff-rides/StaffRideHB_Perryville.pdf"},{"name":"Cassius M. Clay","role":"Kentucky abolitionist and United States major general who advised Wallace on the terrain south of Lexington","side":"United States","source":"https://www.armyupress.army.mil/Portals/7/educational-services/staff-rides/StaffRideHB_Perryville.pdf"},{"name":"William “Bull” Nelson","role":"Kentucky-born major general who replaced Wallace and ordered the improvised army south toward Richmond","side":"United States","source":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=ky007"},{"name":"Charles Cruft","role":"Indiana brigadier general assigned an infantry brigade in the United States Army of Kentucky","side":"United States","source":"https://kynghistory.ky.gov/Our-History/History-of-the-Guard/Documents/ThePaperTrailoftheCivilWarinKY18611865%202.pdf"},{"name":"Mahlon D. Manson","role":"Indiana brigadier general assigned an infantry brigade and command of the column marching to Richmond","side":"United States","source":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=ky007"},{"name":"James S. Jackson","role":"Kentucky-born brigadier general assigned command of the United States cavalry","side":"United States","source":"https://www.armyupress.army.mil/Portals/7/educational-services/staff-rides/StaffRideHB_Perryville.pdf"},{"name":"Braxton Bragg","role":"Army of Mississippi commander who accepted Smith’s separate Kentucky advance and remained at Chattanooga during this episode","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://www.armyupress.army.mil/Portals/7/educational-services/staff-rides/StaffRideHB_Perryville.pdf"},{"name":"Edmund Kirby Smith","role":"Independent East Tennessee commander who bypassed Cumberland Gap and led the Confederate Army of Kentucky toward Lexington","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/ep207-edmund-kirby-smith.jpg","source":"https://www.nps.gov/vick/learn/historyculture/edmund-kirby-smith.htm"},{"name":"Carter L. Stevenson","role":"Commander of Smith’s largest division, detached to contain the United States garrison at Cumberland Gap","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://www.armyupress.army.mil/Portals/7/educational-services/staff-rides/StaffRideHB_Perryville.pdf"},{"name":"Henry Heth","role":"2nd Division commander left near Barbourville when the main Confederate column advanced on August 25","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://www.armyupress.army.mil/Portals/7/educational-services/staff-rides/StaffRideHB_Perryville.pdf"},{"name":"Thomas J. Churchill","role":"Temporary 3rd Division commander who continued north toward Richmond with Smith","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=ky007"},{"name":"Patrick R. Cleburne","role":"4th Division commander leading two brigades borrowed from Bragg’s army toward Richmond","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/ep207-patrick-cleburne.jpg","source":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=ky007"},{"name":"John P. McCown","role":"Division commander left at Knoxville to supervise East Tennessee during Smith’s absence","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://www.armyupress.army.mil/Portals/7/educational-services/staff-rides/StaffRideHB_Perryville.pdf"},{"name":"John S. Scott","role":"Confederate cavalry colonel who screened Smith’s march, fought at Big Hill, and scouted toward Richmond","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://history.ky.gov/markers/route-of-advance"},{"name":"John Hunt Morgan","role":"Kentucky cavalry commander whose July raid and inflated recruiting estimate encouraged the invasion","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/today-in-history/october-08/"}],"losses":[],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/ep207-kirby-smith-kentucky-route.svg","alt":"Schematic map of Kirby Smith’s route from Knoxville through Rogers Gap and Barbourville toward Richmond and Lexington.","caption":"The map separates the main advance from the forces left behind: Stevenson at Cumberland Gap, Heth near Barbourville, and Cleburne, Churchill, and Scott on the Old State Road.","source":"Custom diagram based on U.S. Army, National Park Service, Kentucky Historical Society, and episode transcript sources."},{"image":"/images/ep207-cumberland-gap-operations-map.jpg","alt":"Historical military map of the roads, ridges, fortifications, and 1862 operations around Cumberland Gap.","caption":"A detailed historical map of George W. Morgan’s 1862 operations at Cumberland Gap. The terrain explains why a quick direct assault was unattractive and why Smith chose a route around the position.","source":"U.S. Army Corps of Engineers and Edward Ruger; Library of Congress Geography and Map Division and Library of Virginia."},{"image":"/images/ep207-edmund-kirby-smith.jpg","alt":"Wartime photographic portrait of Confederate Major General Edmund Kirby Smith in uniform.","caption":"Smith’s independent command authority and preference for Lexington turned a preliminary operation into a separate invasion route.","source":"Civil War Glass Negatives and Related Prints, Library of Congress; no known restrictions on publication."},{"image":"/images/ep207-patrick-cleburne.jpg","alt":"Engraved portrait of Confederate Brigadier General Patrick R. Cleburne facing left.","caption":"Cleburne commanded the 4th Division, two brigades borrowed from Bragg, and led one of the formations advancing toward Richmond.","source":"Civil War Photograph Collection, Library of Congress; no known restrictions on publication."}],"sources":[{"label":"Official Civil War Podcast page containing episode 207","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/category/1862/page/5/"},{"label":"U.S. Army Center of Military History: The Civil War in the Western Theater, 1862","url":"https://history.army.mil/portals/143/Images/Publications/catalog/75-7.pdf"},{"label":"U.S. Army staff ride handbook for the Perryville Campaign","url":"https://www.armyupress.army.mil/Portals/7/educational-services/staff-rides/StaffRideHB_Perryville.pdf"},{"label":"National Park Service: Civil War at Cumberland Gap","url":"https://www.nps.gov/cuga/civil-war-at-cumberland-gap.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Edmund Kirby Smith biography","url":"https://www.nps.gov/vick/learn/historyculture/edmund-kirby-smith.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Richmond battle detail","url":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=ky007"},{"label":"Kentucky Historical Society: Route of Advance marker","url":"https://history.ky.gov/markers/route-of-advance"},{"label":"Kentucky General Assembly: Barbourville Civil War actions","url":"https://apps.legislature.ky.gov/LegislativeMoments/moments11RS/web/legislative%20moment%2022.pdf"},{"label":"Library of Congress: 1862 Cumberland Gap operations map","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/lva0000034/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: wartime portrait of Edmund Kirby Smith","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666549/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: portrait of Patrick R. Cleburne","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/93503339/"},{"label":"Official podcast book-recommendations list","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/book-recommendations/"}]}}]}]
12:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section maps-section","id":"maps","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Maps"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"maps-grid","children":[["$","figure","United States operations at Cumberland Gap",{"className":"reference-map","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"map-frame static-map-frame","children":["$","a",null,{"className":"map-image-link","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep207-cumberland-gap-operations-map.jpg","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","aria-label":"Open United States operations at Cumberland Gap at full size","children":["$","img",null,{"src":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep207-cumberland-gap-operations-map.jpg","alt":"Historical military map of Cumberland Gap showing roads, ridges, fortifications, and troop movements during George W. Morgan’s 1862 operations.","width":1600,"height":1200,"loading":"lazy"}]}]}],["$","figcaption",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"United States operations at Cumberland Gap"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Historical map prepared by the U.S. Army Corps of Engineers and Edward Ruger. It shows roads, relief, fortifications, and operations associated with George W. Morgan’s division during 1862. It is a detailed local map, not a map of Smith’s full route to Lexington."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"map-actions","children":[["$","a",null,{"href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep207-cumberland-gap-operations-map.jpg","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","children":["Open full-size map ",["$","$Le",null,{"ref":"$undefined","iconNode":"$d:props:children:1:props:children:0:props:children:1:props:children:2:props:iconNode","className":"lucide-arrow-up-right","size":13}]]}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Library of Congress Geography and Map Division and Library of Virginia; free to use and reuse; see source record below."}]]}],"$undefined"]}]]}],["$","figure","Edmund Kirby Smith",{"className":"reference-map","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"map-frame static-map-frame","children":["$","a",null,{"className":"map-image-link","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep207-edmund-kirby-smith.jpg","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","aria-label":"Open Edmund Kirby Smith at full size","children":["$","img",null,{"src":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep207-edmund-kirby-smith.jpg","alt":"Wartime photographic portrait of Confederate Major General Edmund Kirby Smith in uniform.","width":1600,"height":1200,"loading":"lazy"}]}]}],["$","figcaption",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Edmund Kirby Smith"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Wartime glass-negative portrait of the independent East Tennessee commander whose decision to bypass Cumberland Gap set the campaign’s eastern column in motion."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"map-actions","children":[["$","a",null,{"href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep207-edmund-kirby-smith.jpg","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","children":["Open full-size map ",["$","$Le",null,{"ref":"$undefined","iconNode":"$d:props:children:1:props:children:0:props:children:1:props:children:2:props:iconNode","className":"lucide-arrow-up-right","size":13}]]}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Civil War Glass Negatives and Related Prints, Library of Congress; no known restrictions on publication; see source record below."}]]}],"$undefined"]}]]}],["$","figure","Patrick R. Cleburne",{"className":"reference-map","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"map-frame static-map-frame","children":["$","a",null,{"className":"map-image-link","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep207-patrick-cleburne.jpg","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","aria-label":"Open Patrick R. Cleburne at full size","children":["$","img",null,{"src":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep207-patrick-cleburne.jpg","alt":"Engraved head-and-shoulders portrait of Confederate Brigadier General Patrick R. Cleburne facing left.","w